.Dd November 19, 2026
.Dt HVC 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm hvc ,
.Nm vport
.Nd virtio console
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Sy console Ns = Ns Cm hvc Ns Ar N
.Pp
.Sy console Ns = Ns Cm vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N
.Pp
.Pa /dev/hvc Ns Ar N
.Pp
.Pa /dev/vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N
.Pp
.Pa /dev/virtio-ports/ Ns Ar name
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The kernel drives every virtio console device, QEMU's
.Ql virtio-serial-pci ,
as Linux's
.Ql virtio_console
does: each port is a device of its own in
.Pa /dev ,
and the kernel carries its own console over a port when the boot line
names it.
On such a machine the console needs no serial port: the log, the debug
shell,
.Xr init 8
and every program on
.Pa /dev/console
read and write the port, a page at a time, where a 16550 moves one byte
at a time.
.Pp
The devices are numbered from 0 in the order the PCI bus gives them.
A device has as many ports as its configuration's
.Ql max_nr_ports ,
at most 511, and the host adds and removes them while the machine runs,
which the log records.
Port 0 of a device without the multiport feature, and each port the
device marks as a console, QEMU's
.Ql virtconsole ,
is a console port, named
.Ql hvc Ns Ar N
with the lowest
.Ar N ,
of 0 to 15, that no console port has when the device marks it; a port
keeps its name until it goes.
Every port, generic or console, is also named
.Ql vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N
for port
.Ar N
of device
.Ar D .
Any other port, QEMU's
.Ql virtserialport ,
is a generic port.
.Pp
Each
.Sy console=
on the boot line is read in turn, as Linux reads them.
.Cm hvc Ns Ar N
asks for the console port of that name and
.Cm vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N
for port
.Ar N
of device
.Ar D ,
whatever its kind, which Linux does not offer;
.Cm ttyS0
asks for COM1.
The log goes to every line named, each port once however often it is
named, and the console's input comes from the one named last.
Options after a comma, such as a speed, are not read, and a word that
names neither, such as
.Cm tty0 ,
changes nothing.
Without a
.Sy console=
that names a port the console stays on COM1, as
.Xr console 4
describes it, and the ports are the devices below.
.Pp
A generic port is
.Pa /dev/vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N ,
the character device whose major is device
.Ar D Ns 's
own, 245 for the first, and whose minor is
.Ar N ,
as Linux numbers it, and, when the host names it, also
.Pa /dev/virtio-ports/ Ns Ar name .
It is a stream of bytes to and from the host's end, with no line
discipline.
Its
.Xr open 2
tells the device the guest's end is open, which QEMU waits for before
it gives the port anything, and fails with
.Er EBUSY
while it is open already and with
.Er ENXIO
for a console port.
A
.Xr read 2
gives what the host wrote, waits for it, or with
.Dv O_NONBLOCK
fails with
.Er EAGAIN ;
it returns 0 when nothing waits and the host's end is closed.
A
.Xr write 2
puts what the port's four pages hold and waits while they are the
device's or while the host's end is closed.
.Xr poll 2
gives
.Dv POLLIN
with bytes waiting,
.Dv POLLOUT
with a page free and the host's end open, and
.Dv POLLHUP
while that end is closed.
The last close tells the device the guest's end is closed.
A port removed while open fails its descriptor's calls with
.Er ENODEV .
.Pp
A console port is
.Pa /dev/hvc Ns Ar N ,
character device 229:N, a terminal of its own with a line discipline,
as
.Xr console 4
describes for a terminal, whose input is what the host writes and
whose output goes to the host's end; the port's size is the terminal's
window size.
It is also
.Pa /dev/vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N ,
whose open fails, and a name under
.Pa /dev/virtio-ports
when the host gives it one.
.Pp
A port named becomes a console line when the device announces it,
for which the boot waits at most two seconds; a port announced later
becomes one then.
On a machine with no COM1 the port is then given every record the log
still holds, from the first of the boot, once for the machine's life
and whenever it comes; on a machine with one, the records so far went
to COM1 and the port carries the log from there on.
When the boot line names
.Cm hvc0
first on a machine with COM1, the first device with the emergency write
register carries the log through it from its binding until a port takes
the log; QEMU gives those bytes to its port 0, a console port, while
that port's host's end is open.
While the port has the console's input, an open of its node, or of a
generic port's name under
.Pa /dev/virtio-ports ,
gives the console terminal, as an open of
.Pa /dev/console
does, so a login may be started on either name.
A console port that carries the console's output alone keeps its own
terminal.
.Pp
What is written leaves when a page of it has gathered, at the end of
each record of the log and at the end of each write to the console.
The kernel then waits until the device has taken it, so that a power
off or a reset just after loses nothing.
A host that does not read its end holds the writer for at most 30
seconds; what the four pages in flight cannot hold is then dropped,
and each later write waits 2 milliseconds, until the host reads again.
What the host writes arrives in pages too.
When programs read more slowly than the host writes, the console's
4096 bytes and the port's eight pages fill, and the device stops
reading the host's end until there is room; no byte is dropped.
.Pp
A console port's size, where the device gives one, becomes the console
terminal's window size, and a change of it sends
.Dv SIGWINCH
as
.Dv TIOCSWINSZ
does.
.Pp
A panic that finds another processor stopped inside the port's
transmit path writes through a page of the port's own that no other
writer uses, so that the panic's text reaches the host's end.
One that finds the port's queue itself in use, by the code it
interrupted, writes each byte to the device's emergency write register
instead, where the device has one, which QEMU gives its
.Ql virtconsole
alone.
.Pp
The device is shown as
.Pa /sys/bus/virtio/devices/virtio Ns Ar N ,
bound to
.Ql virtio_console .
When it is unbound through
.Pa /sys/bus/pci/drivers/virtio-pci/unbind ,
or its port is removed, the port's nodes go, its terminal is hung up
and the console goes back to COM1; a port added
again, or the device bound again, takes the console back.
When the port that had the console's input goes, the console terminal
is hung up, as
.Xr vhangup 2
hangs it up: its session is sent
.Dv SIGHUP ,
its readers are woken, and what the port had sent and nothing had read
is dropped.
At shutdown, after the disks, what the ports were given is sent, the
console goes back to COM1, the nodes go and each device is reset, so
that a restart finds it stopped.
A device that the transport can give no interrupt is refused.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/virtio-ports/name"
.It Pa /dev/hvc Ns Ar N
console port's terminal, or the console while the port carries its
input
.It Pa /dev/vport Ns Ar D Ns Cm p Ns Ar N
port
.Ar N
of device
.Ar D ,
or the console while the port carries its input
.It Pa /dev/virtio-ports/ Ns Ar name
the same device as the named port's
.Pa vport
node
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Start QEMU with the console on a generic port, which loses no output
when the reader of the socket falls behind:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
qemu-system-x86_64 -nodefaults -kernel kernel -initrd test.krffs \e
    -chardev socket,id=con,path=con.sock,server=on \e
    -device virtio-serial-pci -device virtserialport,chardev=con \e
    -append console=vport0p1
.Ed
.Pp
Keep the log on COM1 as well, with the port for input:
.Dl console=ttyS0 console=hvc0
.Pp
Add a named port after the boot from QEMU's monitor, and read it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(qemu) chardev-add socket,id=agent,path=agent.sock,server=on,wait=off
(qemu) device_add virtserialport,chardev=agent,name=org.example.agent
$ cat /dev/virtio-ports/org.example.agent
.Ed
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql virtio_console: bound: virtio0: ports with the control queues, at most 4, emergency write
The driver has the device, with what the device offered.
.It Ql virtio_console: port: virtio0: port 3 added
The host added a port;
.Ql removed
says it took one away, and
.Ql is named org.example.agent
gives its name.
.It Ql console: enabled: vport0p1, port 1 of virtio0
The port is the console from here on;
.Ql , for output alone
follows when COM1 was named after it.
.It Ql console: disabled: vport0p1: its port has gone
The console is back on COM1.
.It Ql console: waiting: vport0p1: no device has announced it in 2000 ms
The boot went on without the port, which takes the console when the
device announces it.
.It Ql console: none: console=vport0p1 names no port now; the console stays on COM1
The machine has no virtio console, or the device has no such port.
.It Ql virtio_console: shutdown: virtio0: reset
The device was reset at shutdown;
.Ql the reset did not end
says its memory access was turned off instead.
.It Ql virtio_console: refused: virtio0: no interrupt, which its input needs
The transport found neither MSI-X nor an interrupt line for the device.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr bootlog 4 ,
.Xr console 4 ,
.Xr netconsole 4 ,
.Xr sysfs 5
.Sh STANDARDS
The device is that of the virtio specification, version 1.2, section
5.3, and the driver follows Linux 6.12's
.Pa drivers/char/virtio_console.c
with its hvc console.
As here, Linux gives each console port a terminal of its own and each
generic port a byte device; its
.Cm hvc0
is the first console port that registers, of any device.
Linux names no generic port on the boot line, and its
.Pa /dev/virtio-ports
names are links that udev makes.
.Sh BUGS
A port has no
.Dv O_ASYNC ,
since no file has yet, so its data sends no
.Dv SIGIO .
There is no
.Pa /proc/consoles ,
and the port has no entry under
.Pa /sys/class/tty .
QEMU's
.Ql virtconsole
discards output when its reader falls behind, and its
.Ql virtserialport
does not; use the second for a console whose every byte counts.
